title: History tree and HEAD
tags: [[Event log]]
kind: reference
aliases: head branch
summary: Operations form a tree through their parents; HEAD names the one the data reflects, and a write after a rollback starts a branch.

Each operation names its ''parent'' — the operation it was written from (none for the
root). Within a revision the operations form a chain: the first follows the HEAD before
the revision, each next one the previous; on commit HEAD moves to the last.

```
op_1 [rev 1] ← op_2 [rev 2] ← op_3 [rev 2] ← HEAD
```

After rolling back to `op_1` and writing `op_4`:

```
op_1 ← op_2 ← op_3   ← a branch, still reachable
     └─ op_4           ← HEAD
```

''HEAD'' names the current operation, and the data reflects exactly its state. HEAD and
the data are always consistent: every HEAD move and every data change happen in one
transaction, so a crash leaves both as they were ([[Writes and atomicity]]). A rollback
abandons a branch; it deletes nothing. History is lost in exactly two configured ways:
a prune (`mf log prune`) and the retention limit.

Reading the tree, three views: the ''linear'' line (the root to HEAD), the ''active''
line (that, plus the continuation below HEAD toward the most recent operation — a
rolled-back future, kept visible for redo), and the whole ''tree'' ([[Log endpoints]]).
